            {
                "box": {
                    "id": "obj-71",
                    "linecount": 113,
                    "maxclass": "comment",
                    "numinlets": 1,
                    "numoutlets": 0,
                    "patching_rect": [ 30.0, 553.0, 577.0, 1648.0 ],
                    "text": "GoldComp 4 (4x) — mrelwood plugins\nPage: https://mrelwood5.wixsite.com/plugins\n(GoldComp4 has no individual page on the site; it is listed and distributed\ninside the free mrelwood_PluginPack.)\n\nOnly a sliver less CPU than GoldComp4 set to 4x oversampling.\n\nWhat it is: mrelwood's \"latest go-to compressor for everything\": a stereo\n(or M/S) program compressor with knee, ratio, attack/release, an\nFET-vs-opto release flavor switch, detector HP, RMS-target makeup, negative\n(upward) expansion and a built-in limiter on the output. Parameters use the\noriginal JSFX slider units.\n\nThis copy is the same port with the oversampling selector removed and the\ndetector hard-wired to the source's 4x (linear-phase) oversampled path:\n\n- The oversampling slider (source slider11, 0..6) is gone, so there is\n  nothing to automate or recall — every instance always runs 4x.\n- The codebox only contains the 4x oversampled detector chain. The source's\n  dry (no oversampling), 2x, 2x minimum-phase, 4x minimum-phase, 8x and 8x\n  minimum-phase paths and their state were deleted, along with the selector\n  dispatch. The 2x stage that the 4x path nests internally is kept.\n- Everything else (params, defaults, detector, makeup, limiter) is\n  unchanged from mrelwood_GoldComp4; the calculated makeup gain uses the\n  same constants the original used at slider11 = 3.\n\nParameters (original names; slider1 \"Stereo\", slider2..21 in file):\n\n- slider2  (Stereo: L/R - M/S) 0..1, default 0.5\n  0 = plain stereo processing, 1 = mid/side processing. Beyond ~0.9 the\n  source effectively always uses M/S curves; near 0.5 the crossover is\n  smooth. Musically: M/S on program material, L/R on sparse/single\n  sources.\n\n- slider3  (L/R Link) 0..1, default 0.7\n  How much the two channels' detectors are linked. 0 = fully\n  independent (chases wide images), 1 = fully linked (no image shift).\n  Default 0.7 is a good mix glue setting.\n\n- slider4  (Side thr offset) -15..0, default -6 dB\n  In M/S mode, the amount by which the side channel detector is offset\n  relative to mid. Negative = side compressed earlier/harder, which\n  tightens wide imaging - useful on stems like drum overheads.\n\n- slider5  (Threshold) -48..0, default 0 dB\n  Detector threshold in dBFS. 0 = no compression (limiter off); -6\n  start of normal crushing territory.\n\n- slider6  (Knee) -24..-6, default -12 dB\n  Soft-knee width. Wider (more negative) = a longer, gentler transition\n  into compression; -6 is nearly a hard knee.\n\n- slider7  (Attack) 0.02..50, default 5 ms\n  Attack time per 12 dB of GR (labelled \"ms/12dB\" in the source). Log\n  taper built into the detector: short attacks (<5 ms) let\n  transients through fairly free; long attacks give that classic\n  soft-knee squash. 0.02 ms is essentially instant.\n\n- slider8  (Ratio) 1..12, default 4\n  Compression ratio (ratio 1 = off). Above ~8 approaches hard limiting,\n  below 3 is gentle leveling.\n\n- slider9  (Release) 10..2000, default 120 ms\n  Release time per 12 dB of GR. Pre-120 is quick punch recovery, past\n  500-1000 pumping is slow and continuous (great for glue,\n  dangerous on fast material).\n\n- slider10 (Opto Release, FET - Opto) 0..1, default 1\n  Release character: 0 = FET-style fast/synthetic independent recovery,\n  1 = Opto-style program-dependent slower release. Discrete switch, not\n  tapered.\n\n- slider12 (RMS Target) -36..-6, default -24 dB\n  The original calculated makeup gain target, NOT a new RMS controller\n  (README note). Higher (closer to -6) = very high calculated makeup\n  (louder bass result), lower/-36 keeps output near unity.\n\n- slider13 (Output Trim) -24..+24, default 0 dB\n  Straight output level trim in dB.\n\n- slider15 (Limiter) 0..1, default 1 (off in the source, on in the port)\n  Output limiter stage. NOTE: the source uses 1 = OFF for this control\n  (it is an off/on switch; the port default 1 leaves it off). When on\n  it catches the remaining overshoots of the compressor/limiter path -\n  handy when pushing threshold very low.\n\n- slider16 (Detector HP) 20..1000 Hz, default 20\n  Highpass of the sidechain detector. 20 Hz = full-band detection;\n  50-150 Hz removes the kick/bass from detection so the compressor\n  doesn't over-react to synth bass; 500-1000 turns it into a\n  vocal/brightness-only detector.\n\n- slider17 (Mix) 0..100, default 100 %\n  Dry/wet style parallel blend of compressed and dry audio.\n\n- slider20 (Tilt EQ) -12..+12, default 0 dB\n  Built-in wide tilt shelves on the audio buss (not on the detector).\n  Positive = brighter/tilted up, negative = darker/tilted down. Not a\n  replacement for an EQ - keep it subtle (+/- 1-2 dB).\n\n- slider21 (NegExp, Ratio) 1..4, default 1\n  Negative (upward) expansion ratio. Above 1 it lifts very quiet\n  passages, which makes an interesting \"thinner but detailed\" master.\n  Leave at 1 for a plain compressor; use 2-4 for subtle \"loudrooms\n  dreaminess\" or floor noise lifting.\n\nPort quirks (README notes): the width slider label in the source's GR\ndisplay and its related meters are omitted; slider13 output trim and\nslider12 RMS target control the original calculated makeup. The attdb\nvariable in the source is zero (its assignment line is commented out) and\nits wide tilt shelves, detector HP, negative expansion and post-mix\nlimiter are all retained in the port. slider17 = 0 with limiter off\npasses dry audio.\n",
                    "varname": "paraminfo_comment"
                }
            }
        ],
